  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nDense fog across much of central Minnesota & western Wisconsin \nthis morning has lifted to a bank of stratus clouds that has \nbeen slow to dissipate this afternoon. A sharp contrast in\ntemperatures is present across the area thanks to this cloud\ncover, with temperatures remaining in the 70s under the clouds \n& already round 90 degrees where it has been sunny all morning.\nVery muggy dewpoints in the low to mid 70s will create heat \nindex values near 100 degrees south of the cloud cover from the \nsouthern Twin Cities metro through western Wisconsin & southern \nMinnesota. Elsewhere it will still be warm & sticky, although \ndrier air off of Lake Superior is beginning to advance \nsouthwards through central Minnesota & northwest Wisconsin. \nThis drier air looks to remain in place through tonight, which \nmay limit whether we see another night of widespread fog into \ntomorrow morning. Temperatures will be cooler tonight as well so\nstill expecting some fog across the area, although it may be \nconstrained to more rural & low-lying areas of central Minnesota\n& west-central Wisconsin.\n\nA cluster of thunderstorms is expected to form over the dakotas\novernight, & most high-resilution guidance tracks it through at\nleast far-western Minnesota during the morning hours. Raised \nPoPs tomorrow morning west of the Minnesota River Valley, &\ncan't rule out some gusty winds & small hail with the\nthunderstorms tomorrow morning. Easterly to northeasterly flow \nlooks likely to hold through Saturday, which should help to \nkeep temperatures & dewpoints more comfortable than the last \ntwo days. Cloud cover from the western Minnesota storms could \nkeep temperatures even lower, but for now expect highs in the \nmid-80s with lower dew points in the mid 60s.\n\nAdditional chances for thunderstorms come Saturday night into\nMonday morning, mainly across western Minnesota, with a more \nwidespread chance coming Monday into Tuesday when a more \ndefined shortwave passes through the region. the most likely \nchances for a soaking rain will be closer to the upper level \nforcing across northern Minnesota, but ensemble guidance is at \nleast showing moderate chances for at least 1\" of rain into \ncentral Minnesota & western Wisconsin. Dry weather continues of \nwe miss out on rain chances Monday- Tuesday as cooler & drier \nnorthwesterly flow develops through the rest of the week. \ntemperatures still look to be above-normal however, with highs \nin the upper 70s to low 80s.",
